32
00:00:35,159 --> 00:00:36,679
So, what's on the disc?

33
00:00:37,120 --> 00:00:38,039
Hi there, Nolik!

34
00:00:38,120 --> 00:00:39,200
Hi there, Simka!

35
00:00:39,560 --> 00:00:42,359
It's a cartoon about Gulliver in the land of Lilliput.

36
00:00:42,960 --> 00:00:45,079
My friend Genie let me borrow it.

37
00:00:45,280 --> 00:00:46,760
I have it till tomorrow.

38
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:48,640
And what's the story about?

39
00:00:48,840 --> 00:00:54,000
Well, it's about this guy who gets shipwrecked when people are just so teeny-tiny-tiny.

40
00:00:54,480 --> 00:00:55,640
Fixies, you mean?

41
00:00:55,640 --> 00:00:58,160
No, not Fixies. Lilliputians.

42
00:00:58,280 --> 00:00:59,280
Lilliputians?

43
00:00:59,679 --> 00:01:00,280
Uh-huh.

44
00:01:00,399 --> 00:01:01,359
You know what, Simka?

45
00:01:01,520 --> 00:01:05,200
I think that you Fixies might have come from those Lilliputians.

46
00:01:05,519 --> 00:01:06,560
No way!

47
00:01:06,760 --> 00:01:10,239
Our grandpa told us a completely different story of the Fixies.

48
00:01:14,359 --> 00:01:16,319
When something is very well made,

49
00:01:16,439 --> 00:01:20,599
then the saying goes that it was made with just a little bit of salt.

50
00:01:20,879 --> 00:01:23,960
In old times craftsmen made things to last,

51
00:01:23,959 --> 00:01:29,079
and in each appliance they would leave just a little piece of their soul.

52
00:01:30,079 --> 00:01:35,119
Those little pieces of their soul would turn into tiny craftsmen called Fixies,

53
00:01:35,239 --> 00:01:39,479
who would then make the appliance their home and take care of it every day.

54
00:01:39,599 --> 00:01:42,479
And that's how the very first Fixies came about.

55
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:46,280
But as the years have passed, fewer things are being made by hand,

56
00:01:46,280 --> 00:01:49,719
and more and more things are getting made by machines and factories.

57
00:01:49,839 --> 00:01:53,239
That means there are less and less new Fixies coming from human souls.

58
00:01:53,239 --> 00:01:57,479
Luckily, Fixies can fall in love with each other and have their own family,

59
00:01:57,479 --> 00:01:59,919
raising their children and teaching them well,

60
00:01:59,919 --> 00:02:04,479
so they'll grow up to become skillful and honest master Fixie repairmen.

61
00:02:07,039 --> 00:02:08,359
So you're mistaken.

62
00:02:08,519 --> 00:02:10,120
We're not Lilliputians at all.

63
00:02:10,199 --> 00:02:11,759
We're Fixies!

64
00:02:12,039 --> 00:02:13,599
Yeah, Fixies!

65
00:02:15,879 --> 00:02:18,680
Listen, Tom Thomas, why don't you show us the movie?

66
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:22,039
Yeah, yeah! I want to learn about Lilliputians too!

67
00:02:22,039 --> 00:02:23,239
Really? I do!

68
00:02:23,239 --> 00:02:24,560
Sure, I'll show you.

69
00:02:33,359 --> 00:02:35,639
Oh no! What's going on?

70
00:02:35,799 --> 00:02:37,199
I broke it!

71
00:02:37,560 --> 00:02:40,079
I can't give her back a disc that's messed up.

72
00:02:40,519 --> 00:02:41,519
Don't panic!

73
00:02:41,719 --> 00:02:42,879
We'll take a look at it.

74
00:02:43,159 --> 00:02:44,719
Come lay it down over here.

75
00:02:50,519 --> 00:02:51,479
Tom Thomas!

76
00:02:51,479 --> 00:02:54,079
Why is this disc all covered in jelly?

77
00:02:54,599 --> 00:02:56,319
Because I was touching it with my fingers.

78
00:02:56,439 --> 00:02:57,799
I mean, what else?

79
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:00,759
It's obvious you don't know how a disc works at all!

80
00:03:01,199 --> 00:03:02,599
And you know how it works?

81
00:03:02,759 --> 00:03:04,000
Yeah, I know.

82
00:03:04,439 --> 00:03:05,039
Yeah?

83
00:03:07,879 --> 00:03:11,280
If we take a look at a digital disc through a powerful microscope,

84
00:03:11,519 --> 00:03:14,439
we can see rows of tiny valleys of different lengths.

85
00:03:14,560 --> 00:03:16,639
These valleys are actually a code

86
00:03:16,839 --> 00:03:20,359
for the cartoons, games, or music recorded onto the disc.

87
00:03:20,360 --> 00:03:26,480
Inside a disc player, a laser beam reads the code and helps turn it back into pictures and sounds.

88
00:03:26,680 --> 00:03:29,880
But if you scratch the disc or smudge the disc with dirty fingers,

89
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:33,080
the laser can't read it and the disc won't play!

90
00:03:33,320 --> 00:03:37,000
That's why you need to keep discs clean and stored in cases.

91
00:03:40,440 --> 00:03:43,440
So that's why you should only hold discs along the edges.

92
00:03:43,560 --> 00:03:47,320
And when you're done watching them, you have to put them back inside their boxes.

93
00:03:47,640 --> 00:03:49,240
And what about this one?

94
00:03:49,240 --> 00:03:51,120
Do we have to get rid of it?

95
00:03:51,280 --> 00:03:52,480
Not so fast!

96
00:03:52,600 --> 00:03:55,280
Nolik, this calls for a major cleaning!

97
00:03:55,360 --> 00:03:56,719
Let's get the brooms!

98
00:04:19,240 --> 00:04:26,600
Okay, Tom Thomas, check the disk!

99
00:04:28,199 --> 00:04:29,519
There you go!

100
00:04:29,680 --> 00:04:30,960
Now you're holding it right!

101
00:04:35,280 --> 00:04:37,759
Hooray! The disk works fine!

102
00:04:37,879 --> 00:04:42,160
Hooray! Now we can watch the movie about the Lilliputians!

103
00:04:42,439 --> 00:04:45,480
Hey, Gulliver, why are you sitting there?

104
00:04:45,600 --> 00:04:47,519
You've seen this movie already.

105
00:04:47,759 --> 00:04:48,840
I know and what?

106
00:04:48,839 --> 00:04:51,319
What-what! Look at that pile of disks!

107
00:04:51,479 --> 00:04:53,039
Where do you need to put them?

108
00:04:55,839 --> 00:04:57,039
In their boxes.

